- Terminology and controlled vocabulary work. "Full words, no abbreviations, one name per thing, no synonyms, always singular, most general accurate term" is textbook terminology standardisation (ISO 704 / ISO 1087). Names: Eugen Wüster (general theory of terminology), Juan Sager, and the thesaurus-construction tradition behind ISO 25964.

- Lightweight syntax design. Significant 4-space indentation, no keywords, meaning carried by capitalisation and punctuation. Names: Guido van Rossum (Python's indentation), Clark Evans / Oren Ben-Kiki / Ingy döt Net (YAML), John Gruber (Markdown).

- Universal-language philosophy — and this is where the name comes from. "Alphabet of thought" is Leibniz's alphabetum cogitationum humanarum, the alphabet of human thought underpinning his characteristica universalis. The example in the docs even uses "Gottfried Leibniz" as a value. Predecessors and successors: Ramon Llull (Ars Magna), John Wilkins (philosophical language, 1668), Gottlob Frege (Begriffsschrift), Rudolf Carnap (Aufbau).

- LLM context engineering. The pitch that "AI can do the synthesis with you instead of guessing" plus the name's rhyme with Chain-of-Thought puts it in conversation with Jason Wei (chain-of-thought prompting) and Shunyu Yao (tree of thoughts), and with the broader retrieval/structured-context work.

- If you want the shortest reading list to understand the intellectual core: Sowa's Knowledge Representation for the ontology half, Halpin's Information Modeling and Relational Databases for the fact-modelling half, Zachman's framework papers for the aspects, and Bill Kent's Data and Reality for why any of it is hard.
